# `definition:` — design notes (not yet built)

Syntactic sugar that is a bit more than sugar: it declares a SYMBOL and its
characterizing axiom in one statement, with binder sorts inferred LAZILY.

## Semantics (user rulings, 2026-09-22)

- **Biconditional, not one-directional.** `definition: p(x) when body` gives the
  kernel `axiom: forall x; body iff p(x)`. The predicate is a real, nameable,
  model-mappable symbol AND fully eliminable both ways. (One-directional `=>`
  was considered and rejected: it leaves the predicate underdetermined — nothing
  rules out a model satisfying it everywhere.)
- **Distinct syntax from `define`.** The keyword carries a COLON —
  `definition:` — because `define` (a macro, no identity, not nameable) and
  `definition:` (a declaration + axiom) are close enough in spelling to confuse.
- **Permissive.** "We can generally not reject that much" — if it converts to an
  axiom easily, accept it. No classification of which symbol is being
  "introduced".
- **Binder sorts are INFERRED, LAZILY, and FIXED.** Not inferred at parse time:
  the body is elaborated at FIRST DEMAND, in the demanding context, exactly as
  `define` already works (a define never infers a param sort — the caller's
  already-elaborated argument arrives, and the body's own elaboration types
  everything). The first demand PINS the signature; later uses check against it.
  Fixed, not re-derived per use — re-deriving would be polymorphism, a much
  bigger change in a first-order kernel, and would cost the symbol its identity.

## THE LINT — DROPPED (user, 2026-09-22)

**Decision: do not build a lint that forces `axiom` into `definition:`.** The
analysis below is kept because it is what led to the decision, and because the
shape tests remain useful for anyone triaging the feature later.

**Why it cannot work.** The Peano case is not merely hard, it is a matter of
stance. `addSuccLeft: add(succ(a), b) = succ(add(a, b))` genuinely does two
things at once: it DEFINES `add` by structural recursion, AND it AXIOMATIZES the
arithmetic, because `add` is a primitive of Peano with no prior existence. Those
are the same statement read from two positions, not two textual shapes a checker
could tell apart. Peano with `add` primitive and these as axioms is standard;
Peano with `add` defined by recursion over a bare successor is equally standard.
A lint here asks a question the syntax cannot answer, and it would be wrong on
precisely the cases users care most about. (User: "it is confusing because it
really is conceptually more axiomatic on the system.")

**What survives the objection.** The 14 `iff` sites are definitions under ANY
reading — `invertible(f) iff (exists g; …)` introduces `invertible` and nothing
else; the symbol has no independent existence to take a stance about. But even
there, forcing a rewrite is not worth machinery: `definition:` is shorter and
names the symbol for you, which is incentive enough, and a lint that fires 14
times once and never again earns nothing. Revisit only with evidence that people
are writing the long form by ACCIDENT.

**Design consequence that still holds:** `definition:` should still desugar INTO
an ordinary `axiom` node, because that keeps the feature honest sugar and keeps
everything downstream (models, citations, `--axioms`) working unchanged. The
reusable shape predicate is no longer required, since nothing consumes it.

## (retained) the shape analysis that led there

**Goal: detect an `axiom` that should have been a `definition:`, and force the
rewrite.** We are NOT building this yet, but the implementation must not make it
expensive later.

**Design constraint that follows:** `definition:` must desugar INTO an ordinary
`axiom` AST node, and the shape test must live in ONE named, reusable predicate
(something like `ast.isDefinitional(axiom)`), not inlined in the parser. Then the
lint is that same predicate run over hand-written axioms. If the desugaring and
the detector ever drift apart, the lint reports things the sugar cannot express.

**What the corpus says the test must get right** (scan, 2026-09-22, std/ only):

| shape | count | genuinely definitional? |
|---|---|---|
| `pred(vars) iff body` | 2 | YES — `invertibleDef`, `isUnitCharacterization` |
| `pred(…, f(vars)) iff body` | 12 | YES — the whole set/collection membership style |
| `f(vars) = body` | 63 matched by syntax | only ~6 |

**The bottom row is the trap.** A naive "lhs is a function applied to variables"
test matches `addIsCommutative`, `mulOneLeft`, `iSquared`, `opCommutative` — 57
theory AXIOMS that assert a PROPERTY and introduce nothing. Rewriting those as
definitions would be wrong and the lint would be noise. The genuinely equational
definitions read as such by name and intent: `subDefinition`, `succDefinition`,
`prevDefinition`, `conjDef`, `definitionOfSubtraction`, and arguably recursion
base cases (`foldZero`, `productUpToZero`).

So the syntactic test is NOT sufficient for the equational case.

**CORRECTION (user, 2026-09-22): "a definition does not recurse" is WRONG.** I
wrote that as a heuristic; it is false, and it would reject the clearest
definitions in the library. Recursive definitions are the NORMAL case for a
function on an inductive sort:

    addSuccLeft:  add(succ(a), b) = succ(add(a, b))     // IS the definition of +
    mulSuccLeft:  mul(succ(a), b) = add(mul(a, b), b)   // IS the definition of *
    foldSucc:     foldUpTo(s, succ(k)) = combine(foldUpTo(s, k), at(s, k))
    powerSucc:    power(g, succ(n)) = op(power(g, n), g)
    subSuccSucc:  sub(succ(a), succ(b)) = sub(a, b)

Every one has the head symbol on both sides.

**The real criterion is STRUCTURAL recursion, not absence of recursion.**

    add(succ(a), b) = succ(add(a, b))   definitional: the recursive call's first
                                        argument DECREASES (a under succ(a))
    add(a, b)       = add(b, a)         NOT definitional: the recursive call is on
                                        the SAME arguments, merely rearranged — a
                                        property, not a construction

**But the deeper reason `addIsCommutative` is not a missed definition is
SEMANTIC, not syntactic** (user question, 2026-09-22: "how is add(b,a)=add(a,b)
an axiom and not a theorem?"). The library already answers this, and the split is
principled:

    std/peano.bpa      theorem addIsCommutative   -- `add` HAS a definition
                                                     (addZeroLeft + addSuccLeft),
                                                     so commutativity is PROVED
                                                     by induction
    std/{ring,field,complex,rational,real}.bpa
                       axiom   addIsCommutative   -- `add` is a PRIMITIVE of an
                                                     abstract theory; commutativity
                                                     is part of what it MEANS to be
                                                     a ring, not a consequence

So in the five theories where it is an axiom, **there is nothing to define** —
`add` has no meaning beyond the axioms that constrain it. That is the real
dividing line:

  - a DEFINITION introduces a symbol by giving its meaning in terms of others;
  - a THEORY AXIOM constrains a primitive that has no meaning beyond its axioms.

`mulOneLeft`, `iSquared`, `opCommutative`, `grpIdentityLeft` are all the second
kind. The structural-recursion test above is a usable syntactic PROXY for this
distinction, not the distinction itself — worth remembering when the lint's false
positives are triaged.

This is the right test on the merits, not just a discriminator: it is exactly the
condition that makes the equation WELL-FOUNDED rather than circular.

Revised heuristic for the equational case:
  - lhs is the head symbol applied to arguments that are bare variables or
    CONSTRUCTOR PATTERNS over bare variables (`succ(a)`, not `ONE`);
  - every RHS free variable appears among the lhs variables;
  - every recursive occurrence of the head on the RHS is applied to a
    STRUCTURALLY SMALLER argument than the corresponding lhs pattern.
That accepts all five above, and rejects `addIsCommutative` (same arguments),
`mulOneLeft` (`mul(ONE, a)` — a constant, not a constructor pattern over a
variable) and `iSquared` (`mul(i, i)` — no variables at all).

**A further consequence: definitions come in FAMILIES.** `addZeroLeft` +
`addSuccLeft` together define `add` by cases on the constructor; either alone is
half a definition. A single-axiom `definition:` cannot express a recursive
function at all. So the syntax likely needs a multi-clause form — one statement
carrying the base and step cases — or `definition:` is understood to cover only
the non-recursive shapes, with recursive functions left to hand-written axiom
pairs. UNRESOLVED; decide before building the equational half.

The `iff` rows are safe under the simple test; start the lint there and treat the
equational case as opt-in or advisory until the heuristic is validated against
the 63.

## `--axioms` discloses a definition DIFFERENTLY (user, 2026-09-22)

A definition-sourced axiom is not epistemically the same as a hand-written one,
and the report should not flatten them. `--axioms` answers "what am I trusting?":

  - `extensionality`, `induction` — genuine ASSUMPTIONS about a structure, which
    a reader should weigh;
  - a clause emitted from `definition: sub(a,b) is add(a, neg(b))` — a
    STIPULATION that introduces a symbol. Doubting it is not really coherent.

Both are axioms to the kernel; only the first is something to weigh. Today's
report lists them identically (`std/set/finite.bpa --axioms` shows
`segmentMember` — a pure definition — beside `extensionality` and `induction`).

**Mechanism: MANGLE the emitted axiom's name** (user ruling) rather than keeping a
side table. The marking then travels with the fact, and every consumer sees it
without a lookup. Precedent both ways:

  - the HOLE mechanism publishes as an axiom and distinguishes via the
    `holes_reached` SIDE TABLE — the thing we are avoiding;
  - accelerant synthetics already mangle: `specialize{<hash>}`, `chain{<hash>}`
    (`Prove.zig`). `{}` cannot appear in a parsed identifier, so a mangled name
    can never collide with a user's.

So: emit the axiom under a name the parser could not write, carrying a definition
marker plus the user-facing name, and have the report strip/flag it — listing
definitions separately from assumptions, or annotating them in place.

Consequences to keep in mind when building:
  - the mangled name must still render READABLY wherever an axiom name is shown
    (`--axioms`, `--library`'s unused report, the `Model@theorem` transfer label);
  - `--library`'s "unused axiom" check should probably IGNORE definition-sourced
    axioms, or every unused definition becomes a library error;
  - a definition must still be CITABLE by its user-facing name, so the mangling
    is a publication detail, not a renaming of what the author wrote.

## `by definition` — the citation form (user, 2026-09-22)

A definition emits an axiom, so proofs must cite it. The word is `definition`, on
the **`by` side**: `RuleStr.keyword` partitions the vocabulary by "kernel
primitive → `by`" vs "engine proof-generation → `using`", and a definition
citation generates nothing — the kernel re-checks the cited formula against the
step's claim, exactly as `cite`/`axiom`/`theorem` do.

    @unfold-the-definition |
      exists s: Seq; exists m: Nat; … iff isEvenPerm(sigma)
      [by definition isEvenPerm]

**Multi-arm: `by definition(N) <name>`** cites arm N (user ruling). Each arm is
its own emitted axiom, so each needs its own address, and the parenthesized-argument
form already exists on rule words (`model(M)`, `arithmetic(module)`).

    definition: add(a, b)
      <arm 1>  a is ZERO     ->  b
      <arm 2>  a is succ(k)  ->  succ(add(k, b))

    [by definition(1) add]   // the base clause
    [by definition(2) add]   // the step clause

**Lookup is REMANGLING, not search** (user): the mangled publication name is a
deterministic function of (user-facing name, arm), so a citation just remangles
and looks the fact up directly. No reverse index, no scan, no side table — and
the mangled name is never something an author types or reads.

Resolved by that: `cite` needs no special case. A definition is published ONLY
under its mangled name, so a bare `by cite add` simply misses — the author is
pushed to `by definition` by name resolution, with no extra rule.

RESOLVED — **arms are ZERO-indexed** (corpus survey, 2026-09-22). I had leaned
1-based on the grounds that an author thinks "first clause, second clause". That
was wrong: the corpus is uniformly zero-indexed in the PROOF LANGUAGE, and an arm
index is an index into a list of clauses — the same kind of thing as a sequence
index, not a prose ordinal.

  zero-indexed (everything in the language):
    at(s, ZERO)                     the FIRST sequence entry
    foldUpTo(s, ZERO) = IDENTITY    the empty fold
    foldUpTo(s, succ(k)) = combine(foldUpTo(s, k), at(s, k))
                                    works only because index k is the (k+1)-th entry
    segment(n) = {0, …, n-1}        so hasSize(a, n) counts n elements
    at(r, i) = at(s, sub(n, succ(i)))   today's seqReverse, zero-indexed

  one-indexed — EXACTLY ONE, and not in the language:
    diagnostics render `loc.line + 1`, `loc.column + 1` (diagnostics.zig:62), so
    a user sees `file.bpa:7:17`. Stored zero-indexed; the +1 is a RENDERING
    decision at the last moment, matching the universal editor convention. It
    does not generalize — that is a position in a text file for an external
    convention, not an address into a structure.

  neither: "expects {d} argument(s)" &c. are COUNTS, not ordinals. `--axioms` and
  the hole report address things by NAME, never by position.

So: `[by definition(0) add]` is the base clause, `[by definition(1) add]` the
step.
  - **single-arm form**: bare `by definition add`, or `by definition(1) add`?
    Bare reads better and is the common case; parens would then mark multi-arm
    only. Leaning bare-allowed, with `(N)` required once there is more than one
    arm (an unqualified citation of a multi-arm definition is then an error that
    names the arms).

Implementation notes:
  - add `definition` to `InternPool.RuleStr` (reserved, `by` side). NOTE `of()`
    bounds the reserved range by `.import`, so a new entry goes INSIDE the range
    or that bound moves;
  - the mangled name must render READABLY wherever an axiom name is shown
    (`--axioms`, `--library` unused report, the `Model@theorem` transfer label).

## SYNTAX — settled (user, 2026-09-22)

The keyword is **`definition of`**, and the form SPLITS BY KIND — predicates and
functions get different shapes, because the two cases genuinely differ.

**Predicates** — parenthesized header carrying the binders, then the body. The
head is always the same and the connective is always `iff`, so writing either
would be pure repetition:

    definition of isEvenPerm(g):
      exists s: Seq; exists m: Nat;
        allTranspositions(s, m) and productUpTo(s, m) = g and divides(TWO, m)

    -> pred isEvenPerm(g: G)
     + axiom <mangled>: forall g: G; <body> iff isEvenPerm(g)

**Functions** — bare header naming only the symbol, then one or more ARMS, each
written EXACTLY as the axiom it becomes. For a recursive function neither the
head nor the body is constant, and that variation IS the definition:

    definition of sub:
      sub(a, b) = add(a, neg(b))

    definition of add:
      add(ZERO, b) = b;
      add(succ(k), b) = succ(add(k, b))

    -> func add(…): …
     + axiom <mangled 0>: forall b: Nat; add(ZERO, b) = b
     + axiom <mangled 1>: forall k, b: Nat; add(succ(k), b) = succ(add(k, b))

A one-arm function is just a function with one arm — NOT a special form. `sub`
and `add` are written the same way.

**Why the asymmetry is right:** it mirrors a real one. A predicate definition has
ONE shape with a fixed head; a function definition may have SEVERAL shapes whose
differing heads are the whole content. Writing `isEvenPerm(g) iff …` would repeat
what the header already said; writing `add`'s arms is unavoidable.

**Parse-time distinction:** the header's parens. `definition of p(args):` is a
predicate and takes a body; `definition of f:` is a function and takes arms. No
lookahead needed.

### Clause GUARDS — Erlang-inspired, `when` after the head (user, 2026-09-22)

A function arm may carry a guard, placed Erlang-style on the CLAUSE (after the
head) rather than on the symbol:

    definition of div:
      div(a, b) = …  when b != ZERO

    definition of gcd:
      gcd(a, b) = a                  when b = ZERO;
      gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, mod(a, b))  when b != ZERO

This solves a scoping problem the bare header otherwise has: the guard mentions
`b`, and clause-placement means `b` is bound by the clause head right there. A
symbol-level guard (`definition of div requires b != ZERO:`) has no binder list
to scope against, and adding one to the header would collide with the rule that
parens mark a PREDICATE.

`requires` already exists for this on `func` declarations (`func div(a: Nat, b:
Nat): Nat requires b != ZERO`); a definition's guard is the per-clause analogue.
Predicates need none — a predicate is total on its argument sorts.

**ALL CONDITIONS EXPLICIT ON ALL CLAUSES (user ruling).** Erlang clauses are
ordered and first-match-wins; axioms have NO precedence. Two unguarded clauses
that overlap are simply inconsistent — `gcd(a, ZERO) = a` beside an unguarded
`gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, mod(a, b))` both apply at `b = ZERO` and contradict. So every
clause states its own condition in full, inheriting nothing from clause order.

Consequences of that:
  - clauses are SEMANTICALLY order-independent; reordering them changes nothing.
    `definition(N)` is therefore POSITIONAL ADDRESSING, not precedence.
  - the alternative — emitting each clause guarded by the negation of the earlier
    ones — was rejected: it makes the emitted axioms differ from what the author
    wrote, which breaks the property that an arm IS its axiom.
  - DISJOINTNESS / EXHAUSTIVENESS stay the author's responsibility, unchecked,
    exactly as they are today for hand-written axiom pairs. The sugar does not
    make this worse, but it does make an overlapping clause set easier to write
    by accident. A future check could verify the guards are pairwise exclusive.

  - PATTERNS vs GUARDS: with all conditions explicit, `gcd(a, ZERO) = a` and
    `gcd(a, b) = a when b = ZERO` say the same thing. Allow BOTH — `add(succ(k),
    b)` is far more readable than `add(n, b) when n = succ(k)`, which would also
    leave `k` unbound. Note that head patterns are a genuinely NEW concept for
    the language (nothing destructures a term today; proofs case-split with
    `zeroOrSucc` + `or_elim`), so this is the one part of the feature that is
    more than sugar.

### Clause GUARDS — Erlang-inspired, `when` after the head (user, 2026-09-22)

A function arm may carry a guard, placed Erlang-style on the CLAUSE (after the
head) rather than on the symbol:

    definition of gcd:
      gcd(a, b) = a                  when b = ZERO;
      gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, mod(a, b))  when b != ZERO

Clause placement solves a scoping problem the bare header otherwise has: the
guard mentions `b`, and `b` is bound by the clause head right there. A
symbol-level guard (`definition of gcd requires …:`) has no binder list to scope
against, and adding one would collide with the rule that parens mark a PREDICATE.
`requires` already does this job on `func` declarations; a clause guard is the
per-clause analogue. Predicates need none (total on their argument sorts).

**ALL CONDITIONS EXPLICIT ON ALL CLAUSES (user ruling).** Erlang clauses are
ordered and first-match-wins; axioms have NO precedence. Two overlapping
unguarded clauses are simply inconsistent. So every clause states its condition
in full and inherits nothing from clause order. Therefore:
  - clauses are SEMANTICALLY order-independent; `definition(N)` is POSITIONAL
    ADDRESSING, not precedence;
  - emitting each clause guarded by the negation of earlier ones was REJECTED —
    it would make the emitted axiom differ from what the author wrote, breaking
    the property that an arm IS its axiom;
  - disjointness/exhaustiveness stay the author's responsibility, unchecked,
    exactly as for hand-written axiom pairs today.

### NO pattern matching — and none is needed (user, 2026-09-22)

I had flagged "head patterns" as the one part of the feature that is more than
sugar. **That was a misreading**: I saw Erlang-shaped syntax and assumed Erlang
semantics. `add(succ(k), b)` is NOT a pattern — it is an ordinary TERM. `k` is a
normal universally quantified variable and `succ(k)` is a plain application in
argument position. Nothing destructures.

This is already how the corpus writes these axioms:

    axiom addSuccLeft: forall a, b: Nat; add(succ(a), b) = succ(add(a, b))
    axiom foldSucc:    forall s: Seq; forall k: Nat;
                       foldUpTo(s, succ(k)) = combine(foldUpTo(s, k), at(s, k))
    axiom powerSucc:   forall g: Grp; forall n: Int; power(g, succ(n)) = op(power(g, n), g)

So an arm is just a plain equation over universally quantified variables, the
binders come from the variables appearing in it (the same lazy inference already
settled for predicates), and the feature introduces NO new concept — it is sugar
end to end.

Consequently the choice is not "patterns vs guards" but simply what the author
writes, both being legal axiom text already:

    definition of add:            -- distinct heads: disjointness is VISIBLE
      add(ZERO, b) = b;
      add(succ(k), b) = succ(add(k, b))

    definition of gcd:            -- same head: a guard states the distinction
      gcd(a, b) = a                  when b = ZERO;
      gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, mod(a, b))  when b != ZERO

**Consequences:**
  - ARMS ARE ONLY EVER FUNCTIONS, so `definition(N)` indexing applies only to
    function definitions. A predicate has exactly one body and is cited BARE:
    `[by definition isEvenPerm]`. That settles the bare-citation question —
    bare for predicates, `(N)` for functions.
  - The predicate body's connective is IMPLIED (`iff`; a predicate body is always
    a proposition). Function arms write `=` themselves, as they must anyway.
  - Arms are ZERO-INDEXED (see above): `[by definition(0) add]` is the base
    clause, `[by definition(1) add]` the step.
  - Keywords needed: `definition` and `of`. `when` is NOT needed and should be
    un-reserved (it was reserved in 2d81b87 for an earlier design of mine).
    `of` is free — the only corpus hits are English prose in literate markdown,
    outside bpa blocks — and `from` sets the precedent for a small word as a
    keyword (`unpack r: Seq from the-exists`).

